messages:
sam: Hey — running into flaky ObjectDisposedException when combining a long-lived HttpClient (via IHttpClientFactory), ResponseHeadersRead streaming, and Polly retries. Pattern: Request -> read response stream with StreamReader -> sometimes read fails halfway and Polly retries the whole handler chain, but the response stream was already consumed/disposed. Any recommended patterns?

anna: Short: you can't safely retry after you've started consuming a network stream unless you buffer or the request is idempotent and can be restarted. Options: 1) buffer the response (MemoryStream) for small payloads, 2) avoid retries for read-time failures, 3) do retries only around the Send for transient socket failures. :thinking_face:

maria: Also: make sure you create a new HttpRequestMessage for every retry and don't reuse Content streams. Polly will re-send the same request instance unless you clone it. We have a small helper we use: a Clone extension that copies headers and buffers Content into ByteArrayContent so it's rewindable. Example:

```csharp
// simplistic clone (async read for content)
public static async Task<HttpRequestMessage> CloneAsync(this HttpRequestMessage req)
{
    var clone = new HttpRequestMessage(req.Method, req.RequestUri);
    if (req.Content != null)
    {
        var bytes = await req.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync();
        clone.Content = new ByteArrayContent(bytes);
        foreach (var h in req.Content.Headers) clone.Content.Headers.TryAddWithoutValidation(h.Key, h.Value);
    }
    foreach (var h in req.Headers) clone.Headers.TryAddWithoutValidation(h.Key, h.Value);
    return clone;
}
```

This lets Polly re-send, but note it buffers the request body, not the response.

jordan: If you're streaming large responses and can't buffer, the pragmatic approach is: fail fast on send when you can, and only retry on transient network/send errors. For read-time errors (mid-stream) you either need the server to support resumable ranges or accept that you can't transparently retry. We also tune SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime so the TCP connection doesn't become stale. Example config: `new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5) }`.

sam: That helps. A follow-up: how do people distinguish send vs read errors in a DelegatingHandler so Polly only retries sends? Right now our retry wraps the whole SendAsync pipeline.

anna: One pattern: put a small DelegatingHandler before the point you read the response body that catches exceptions thrown during `SendAsync` (i.e., before returning the response content). But since SendAsync returns a response message, anything that happens while downstream code reads the stream is outside the scope of SendAsync. So instead: keep your retry policy around the `client.SendAsync(request, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead)` call only, not around the code that consumes the stream. Example:

```csharp
// retry only the send and header read
var response = await retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() =>
    httpClient.SendAsync(reqClone, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct));
// then separately read response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync()
using var stream = await response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
// stream processing here - no automatic retry
```

maria: Yup. Also consider using `Policy.Handle<SocketException>()` or `HttpRequestException` and avoid retrying on `IOException` coming from the stream consumer.

sam: Great — we have one more constraint: we can't buffer full response bodies ( >100MB). For resumable downloads we might implement ranged GETs on server side, but not supported yet. For now I'll: retry only on send failures, and add a clear log + metric for mid-stream failures so product can decide.

jordan: If you want a small safety net, you can stream directly into a temp file (FileStream) rather than memory; that's buffering but not memory-bound. Then if you need to retry, you can delete and restart. Not transparent but often acceptable.

devex-bot: :information_source: KB: https://wiki.redwood.internal/devex/httpclient-retries-and-streaming — checklist: use IHttpClientFactory, clone requests for retries, only retry on send, avoid reusing stream-backed content, consider temp-file buffering.

sam: Thanks everyone — implementing request cloning + send-only retry wrapper first. Will post follow-up if we run into more noise. :thumbsup:
